  ('q3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000358', 'e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 's3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 't3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', 'PYQ', '[SSC CGL 2016 Tier-1] In a certain code language, if ''FLOWER'' is coded as ''UOLDVI'', how will ''GARDEN'' be coded in that same system?', 'TZIVWM', 'TYIWVM', 'TZIWVM', 'SZIWVM', 'C', '{"why":"Each letter is paired with its opposite alphabet (G<->T, A<->Z, R<->I, D<->W, E<->V, N<->M), producing TZIWVM.","concept":"Alphabetical Reverse Positional Opposites (Sum = 27)","exam_perspective":"SSC CGL emphasizes high speed, standard shortcut rules, and direct grammar patterns.","remember":"Opposite letter pairs sum up to 27 (e.g. A=1 + Z=26 = 27).","related_concept":"Letter coding, Circular alphabet shifts"}'::jsonb, 'easy', 2016, 'SSC CGL Tier-1 Examination', 'approved'),
